o build my formation to counter the opponent. Also, there are no strategic weaknesses and it is a quiet move. (D, 7) and (G,4) can open more vulnerabilities.\n\n**After careful comparison, (F, 2) stands out** because it's a quiet move with low flip count, it's internal and it won't allow my opponent to get into a good place. It also helps preserve my mobility.\n\n**(B, 7) is adjacent to A8.** It is also a corner square to avoid, and the opponent has many options. So it would not be a smart play.\n\n**(G, 7) is adjacent to H8.** So it's an X-square. No good.\n\nI need to focus on what to do, not what not to do. **So I should recommend (F, 2).**\n\n\n", 'output': 'Situation: The game is in the mid-game phase. I (playing \'*\') hold a strong horizontal line in the center-left (row D), while the opponent (\'@\') controls the row below (row E) and a vertical segment in column 6. Several valid moves are available, including moves that extend to the edges or the 7th column/row, and some internal moves.\n\nEvaluation:\n1.  **(F, 2)**: This is a quiet, internal move. It flips only one opponent disc (E3), which helps minimize the opponent\'s mobility in the next turn while strengthening my control of the center. It does not place a piece on dangerous squares (like X-squares or C-squares) and keeps the game \'tight\'.\n2.  **(G, 4)**: This move flips two discs (F4, E4) and centralizes well. However, it places a piece on row G (the 7th row), which is closer to the edge and can sometimes be a stepping stone for the opponent to attack the corners or edges.\n3.  **(B, 7)** and **(G, 7)**: These are X-square moves (diagonally adjacent to the corners A8 and H8 respectively). Playing these usually gives the opponent a strong chance to capture a corner, so they should be avoided.\n4.  **(D, 7)**: This move creates a solid wall of my pieces from D1 to D7. Creating long walls is generally a strategic error in Othello/Reversi because it provides the opponent with many stable anchoring positions to flip my pieces.\n\nReason: **(F, 2)** is the most strategically sound choice. It follows the principle of "evaporation" (minimizing one\'s own disc count early on) and restricts the opponent\'s options. It is a safe, internal move that improves my position without taking unnecessary risks on the edges or near the corners.\n\nAnswer: (F, 2)'}
