y G7, and have a piece that becomes part of the board, the board has 10 spaces total. The opponent goes last. Does this give up too much in terms of my plan to dominate?\n\nI'm thinking about parity again. If I do that, the empty spaces remaining is 10. If I go, then 9. Opponent, 8. Me 7. Opponent 6. Then, me 5, opponent 4. And so on. And, with me starting, the last move ends with opponent. Is there an advantage there or just control?\n\nIt is the edge moves, and the control that are the primary advantage.\n\nI look at that column on 1 and it is safe, so I stick with (C,1) and make it the best move.\n\n**Final Decision**\n\n(C,1) is the winner. It solidifies a whole edge, removes my opponent's options, and is a very safe play that keeps me in the position to win the match. I'm going with that.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Situation:\nI currently hold a dominating position, possessing all four corners (A1, A8, H1, H8). The game is in the final stage with only 10 empty squares remaining. My primary objective is to maximize my stable disc count and restrict the opponent\'s mobility, ensuring a comfortable win. The opponent\'s pieces are clustered in the center and the right side of the board.\n\nEvaluation:\n- **(C, 1)**: This move is located on the left edge. Placing a piece here sandwiches the opponent\'s piece at B1 between my new piece at C1 and my corner at A1. Additionally, since I already own D1 through H1 and the corner A1, filling C1 and flipping B1 will result in the entire Column 1 (A1 to H1) becoming my pieces. These are stable discs that can never be flipped back. This move is extremely safe and denies the opponent access to the left edge.\n- **(G, 7)**: This move is aggressive and flips a significant number of pieces along Row 7, Column 7, and the diagonal. However, by flipping F7 to my color, it might grant the opponent a move at G8 (sandwiching F7 against E6). Since G8 is adjacent to my corner H8, it is not fatal, but allowing the opponent a move in that pocket i